000100 IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
000200 PROGRAM-ID. ADVANCE.
000300* WRITE ... ADVANCING, IBM's way: the record's own first byte,
000400* which the program reserves, carries machine carriage control.
000500* AFTER n is immediate spacing records -- three lines at a time
000600* -- then the line written without spacing (X'01'); AFTER 0 has a
000700* no-op (X'03') first; BEFORE n is the line with write-and-space
000800* (X'09', X'11', X'19'), and immediate spacing for more than
000900* three; a channel is skip-immediate after (X'8B') or write-and-
001000* skip before (X'89'). A WRITE with no ADVANCING is BEFORE 1. The
001100* program writes the file, reads it back with the control byte as
001200* data, and shows each code as a number. The text of a spacing-
001300* only record is whatever the buffer held, so it is not shown.
001400* Expected output recorded from IBM ANS COBOL on TK5.
